.TH BRANDELF 1 2026-09-25 "Substrate" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
brandelf \- show or set the OS/ABI brand of ELF files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B brandelf
.RB [ \-v ]
.RB [ \-f
.IR abi_number " |"
.B \-t
.IR brand ]
.I file
\&...
.br
.B brandelf
.B \-l
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B brandelf
reads or rewrites the
.B EI_OSABI
byte of the ELF identification header of each
.IR file .
The Substrate kernel uses this byte to choose the personality under
which an executable runs; binaries built for Substrate carry
.B ELFOSABI_SUBSTRATE
(64).
.PP
Without
.B \-f
or
.BR \-t ,
each
.I file
is opened read-only and a line of the form
.PP
.EX
File 'a.out' is of brand 'Substrate' (64).
.EE
.PP
is printed to standard output.
With
.B \-f
or
.BR \-t ,
each file is opened read-write and the byte is overwritten in place;
nothing is printed.
Only the 16-byte identification header is read and written; the rest
of the file is not checked.
.PP
This is FreeBSD's
.B brandelf
with a Substrate brand added.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-f " abi_number"
Set the brand to the numeric value
.IR abi_number ,
which must be between 0 and 255.
The number is converted with
.BR atoi (3),
so a non-numeric argument is taken as 0.
Cannot be combined with
.BR \-t .
.TP
.B \-l
Print the list of known brand names and their values to standard
error and exit with status 0.
Any
.I file
arguments are ignored.
.TP
.BI \-t " brand"
Set the brand by name.
Names are matched without regard to case:
.RS
.PD 0
.TP 12
.B FreeBSD
9
.TP
.B Linux
3
.TP
.B Solaris
6
.TP
.B SVR4
0
.TP
.B Substrate
64
.TP
.B SysV
0
.PD
.RE
.IP
An unknown name is an error; the list of known names is printed and
.B brandelf
exits.
Cannot be combined with
.BR \-f .
.TP
.B \-v
Accepted for compatibility and ignored.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
A usage error, an unknown brand, no
.I file
arguments, or at least one
.I file
could not be opened, was not an ELF file, or could not be written.
Remaining files are still processed after a per-file error.
.SH EXAMPLES
Show the brand of a binary:
.PP
.EX
$ brandelf /bin/ls
File '/bin/ls' is of brand 'Substrate' (64).
.EE
.PP
Mark a binary as a Linux executable:
.PP
.EX
$ brandelf \-t Linux ./prog
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR readelf (1),
.BR elfedit (1),
.BR ld (1)
.SH HISTORY
.B brandelf
first appeared in FreeBSD 2.2.
.SH BUGS
A brand byte of 128 or greater is printed as a very large number,
because the header is held in a signed
.B char
buffer.
